#gf180mcuD - a starter design-rule deck for GDS Viewer.
#
#NOT A SIGNOFF DECK, AND NOT AFFILIATED WITH GLOBALFOUNDRIES. This is a hand-transcribed subset for catching
#obvious mistakes while looking at a layout. A design is signed off against the PDK's own decks - KLayout's in
#gf180mcu_fd_pr/rules/klayout/drc, and Magic's in open_pdks - not against this.
#
#Layer numbers are from the drawn-layer table of the GF180MCU design manual, and every value from its rule
#tables - docs/physical_verification/design_manual/tables_clear in github.com/google/gf180mcu-pdk, published at
#https://gf180mcu-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/latest/physical_verification/design_manual/design_manual.html
#
#THREE THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TRUSTING A NUMBER HERE
#
#**The values are the 3.3 V ones.** Most rules give a second, larger value for the 5 V and 6 V devices drawn
#under Dualgate - COMP spacing is 0.28 at 3.3 V and 0.36 at 5 V and 6 V. The smaller is what is checked here,
#everywhere, so a 6 V device drawn too close by its own rule passes; but nothing is reported that the manual
#allows.
#
#**This is the five-metal stack, gf180mcuD**, whose top metal is Metal5, 11K Angstrom thick. In a five-metal stack
#Metal5 "must comply with MetalTop rule" (design manual 2.8) - 0.44 wide and 0.46 apart at 9K and 11K (7.15), not
#the 0.28 of the metals under it. The tech LEF the standard cells ship for this stack,
#gf180mcu_5LM_1TM_11K_7t_tech.lef, says the same.
#
#Values are DATABASE UNITS, which for GF180 is nanometers: the micron figure in the manual times 1000. Areas are
#square nanometers, so 0.1444 um2 is 144400. Nothing is scaled and nothing is rounded.

#Drawn layers ***********************************************************************

layer DNWELL    12/0
layer Nwell     21/0
layer LVPWELL  204/0
layer COMP      22/0
layer Dualgate  55/0
layer Poly2     30/0
layer Nplus     32/0
layer Pplus     31/0
layer SAB       49/0
layer Contact   33/0
layer Metal1    34/0
layer Via1      35/0
layer Metal2    36/0
layer Via2      38/0
layer Metal3    42/0
layer Via3      40/0
layer Metal4    46/0
layer Via4      41/0
layer Metal5    81/0

#Derived layers *********************************************************************
#
#A contact lands on COMP or on Poly2, never on both - CO.10 forbids one on a gate - and each is enclosed by what
#it lands on, by its own rule. Checked as one layer, every contact on poly would fail COMP's enclosure and every
#contact on COMP would fail Poly2's.
#
#**Read by no rule below, for now.** CO.3 and CO.4, the two enclosures they are for, are in the list at the foot of
#this file - see there for why. They are kept because they are right, and cost nothing while no rule reaches them.

derive compcont = Contact and COMP
derive polycont = Contact and Poly2

#Rules ******************************************************************************
#
#id           check      operands            value   description

#Wells. A space under the equipotential limit is always wrong - the manual says to merge the two - so that is the
#limit checked; two wells at different potentials need more, which only a netlist can say.
rule DN.1       width      DNWELL             1700   "DNWELL width"
rule DN.2a      space      DNWELL             2500   "DNWELL spacing"
rule NW.1a      width      Nwell               860   "Nwell width"
rule NW.2a      space      Nwell               600   "Nwell spacing"
rule LPW.1      width      LVPWELL             600   "LVPWELL width"
rule LPW.2b     space      LVPWELL             860   "LVPWELL spacing"

#COMP. DF.10's "field area" is the field COMP closes round, which is a hole in the merged layer.
rule DF.1a      width      COMP                220   "COMP width"
rule DF.3a      space      COMP                280   "COMP spacing"
rule DF.9       area       COMP             202500   "COMP min area"
rule DF.10      holearea   COMP             260000   "Field enclosed by COMP, min area"

#The thick gate oxide
rule DV.2       space      Dualgate            440   "Dualgate spacing"
rule DV.5       width      Dualgate            700   "Dualgate width"

#Poly2
rule PL.1       width      Poly2               180   "Poly2 interconnect width"
rule PL.3a      space      Poly2               240   "Poly2 spacing"

#Implants
rule NP.1       width      Nplus               400   "Nplus width"
rule NP.2       space      Nplus               400   "Nplus spacing"
rule NP.8a      area       Nplus            350000   "Nplus min area"
rule PP.1       width      Pplus               400   "Pplus width"
rule PP.2       space      Pplus               400   "Pplus spacing"
rule PP.8a      area       Pplus            350000   "Pplus min area"

#Salicide block
rule SB.1       width      SAB                 420   "SAB width"
rule SB.2       space      SAB                 420   "SAB spacing"

#Contact. CO.6 is the least Metal1 may cover a contact by anywhere. The 0.06 the manual asks for at the end of a
#narrow line, and beside a side covered by less than 0.04, depends on finding that end or that side, which a
#region check cannot do.
rule CO.1       width      Contact             220   "Contact size"
rule CO.2a      space      Contact             250   "Contact spacing"
rule CO.6       enclosure  Contact   Metal1      5   "Metal1 overlap of contact"

#Metal stack
rule M1.1       width      Metal1              230   "Metal1 width"
rule M1.2a      space      Metal1              230   "Metal1 spacing"
rule M1.3       area       Metal1           144400   "Metal1 min area"
rule M2.1       width      Metal2              280   "Metal2 width"
rule M2.2a      space      Metal2              280   "Metal2 spacing"
rule M2.3       area       Metal2           144400   "Metal2 min area"
rule M3.1       width      Metal3              280   "Metal3 width"
rule M3.2a      space      Metal3              280   "Metal3 spacing"
rule M3.3       area       Metal3           144400   "Metal3 min area"
rule M4.1       width      Metal4              280   "Metal4 width"
rule M4.2a      space      Metal4              280   "Metal4 spacing"
rule M4.3       area       Metal4           144400   "Metal4 min area"

#The top metal, by the MetalTop rules
rule MT.1       width      Metal5              440   "Top metal (Metal5) width"
rule MT.2a      space      Metal5              460   "Top metal (Metal5) spacing"
rule MT.4       area       Metal5           562500   "Top metal (Metal5) min area"

#Vias. The metal above a via covers it by 0.01 on every side, and so does the metal below it from Metal2 up.
rule V1.1       width      Via1                260   "Via1 size"
rule V1.2a      space      Via1                260   "Via1 spacing"
rule V1.4       enclosure  Via1      Metal2     10   "Metal2 overlap of Via1"
rule V2.1       width      Via2                260   "Via2 size"
rule V2.2a      space      Via2                260   "Via2 spacing"
rule V2.3       enclosure  Via2      Metal2     10   "Metal2 overlap of Via2"
rule V2.4       enclosure  Via2      Metal3     10   "Metal3 overlap of Via2"
rule V3.1       width      Via3                260   "Via3 size"
rule V3.2a      space      Via3                260   "Via3 spacing"
rule V3.3       enclosure  Via3      Metal3     10   "Metal3 overlap of Via3"
rule V3.4       enclosure  Via3      Metal4     10   "Metal4 overlap of Via3"
rule V4.1       width      Via4                260   "Via4 size"
rule V4.2a      space      Via4                260   "Via4 spacing"
rule V4.3       enclosure  Via4      Metal4     10   "Metal4 overlap of Via4"
rule V4.4       enclosure  Via4      Metal5     10   "Metal5 overlap of Via4"

#Every coordinate against the manufacturing grid, 5 nm on GF180: "Consistent layout all designs on a 0.005um grid"
#(design manual 3.4), and the off-grid checks of the PDK's own KLayout deck. Stated rather than read back off the
#file, for the reason sky130A.drc gives.
rule grid.1     offgrid    *                     5   "Coordinate off the 5 nm manufacturing grid"

#Rules deliberately NOT here *********************************************************
#
#Real GF180 rules this checker cannot express, or that are not transcribed yet - listed so their absence is a
#decision on the record rather than an oversight.
#
#CO.3     Poly2 over a contact, 0.07, and
#CO.4     COMP over a contact, 0.07. In this deck until they were run over the bundled GF180 cells, and taken out
#         for what they reported there: three signed-off cells - mux2_1, oai22_1 and or2_1 - each failing by a sliver
#         5 or 10 nm deep at an inside corner of the COMP or the poly. The geometry is fine. In mux2_1 the contact's corner
#         is 71.6 nm from the COMP's inside corner, which a 70 nm rule passes measured as distance - the way the PDK's
#         own KLayout deck measures it, `euclidian`. An enclosure here is measured by growing the contact as a
#         square, and the square's corner reaches into the notch. The edge engine measures as distance, but width
#         and spacing only, and refuses an enclosure that names a metric. Until it measures one, these would report
#         the ordinary corners of GlobalFoundries' own cells - 48 of them in one eFPGA tile - so they are not run.
#
#V1.3     Metal1 over Via1. The least the manual allows is 0.00 - a via may sit flush with Metal1's edge - and
#         a limit of zero measures nothing, so the deck refuses it. The 0.06 asked for at the end of a narrow
#         line depends on finding the end, as CO.6's does.
#PL.2     Gate length, 0.28 at 3.3 V. It is a width along the channel only, and the gate's other dimension, the
#         channel width, may be as small as 0.22 - a width check of the gate would report every narrow
#         transistor. DIRECTIONAL, like poly.7 and poly.8 in sky130A.drc.
#PL.4     Poly2 end cap beyond COMP, 0.22, and
#DF.6     COMP beyond the gate, 0.24. Both directional, the same way.
#Mn.2b    Space to wide metal, 0.30, and MT.2b, 0.60: the limit depends on the width of the metal beside the gap,
#         which neither engine measures.
#NP.3 to NP.5, PP.3 to PP.5
#         The implants' spaces and overlaps to COMP and the gates: two dozen cases conditioned on the wells and on
#         how far a COMP sits from a well edge. Transcription work, not done.
#DF.13, DF.14
#         The furthest a transistor may be from a tap, 20 um at 3.3 V: a latch-up rule over distances, not shapes.
#PL.8, Mn.4
#         Poly2 at least 14% and each metal at least 30% of the die. Expressible as density, and wrong on anything
#         smaller than a die - a cell fails it however it is drawn.
#The 5 V and 6 V values
#         Under Dualgate most limits are larger. Checking them takes the rule twice, once inside Dualgate and once
#         outside, which `except` can say - and is not written yet.
